WEBVTT

00:00:00.361 --> 00:00:03.447
Last video ended here: the sine curve, with

00:00:03.648 --> 00:00:05.732
x in radians, and a tangent at the

00:00:05.792 --> 00:00:06.093
origin.

00:00:06.855 --> 00:00:08.891
Can we read its gradient straight from the

00:00:08.912 --> 00:00:09.214
graph?

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.168
Draw the line y equals x.

00:00:12.498 --> 00:00:14.684
The tangent lies right along it, so the

00:00:14.725 --> 00:00:16.711
slope there looks like exactly one.

00:00:17.476 --> 00:00:18.300
Now look at the peak.

00:00:18.904 --> 00:00:20.731
The height is one, but the curve is

00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:21.173
flat.

00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:23.823
So the gradient isn't the same as the

00:00:23.885 --> 00:00:24.105
height.

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:27.116
What function gives the gradient of sine at

00:00:27.297 --> 00:00:27.919
every x?

00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:30.430
Check that first slope properly.

00:00:31.296 --> 00:00:32.837
One point can't give a slope on its

00:00:32.958 --> 00:00:35.542
own, so, as in Year 11, take a

00:00:35.643 --> 00:00:37.726
second point on the curve, just to the

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:40.070
right of zero, and find the gradient of

00:00:40.091 --> 00:00:40.551
the chord.

00:00:41.313 --> 00:00:43.117
With the point at x equals a half,

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:45.562
it's about point nine six.

00:00:46.366 --> 00:00:48.922
At point one, about point nine nine eight.

00:00:49.688 --> 00:00:51.878
Bring it closer still, and the gradient settles

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.346
on exactly one.

00:00:54.332 --> 00:00:56.902
So, with x in radians, the sine curve

00:00:57.064 --> 00:00:59.152
leaves the origin with slope one.

00:00:59.821 --> 00:01:02.170
Now read the slope at the other landmarks.

00:01:02.793 --> 00:01:05.178
At pi over two, the peak, the curve

00:01:05.259 --> 00:01:07.123
is flat, so the slope is zero.

00:01:07.846 --> 00:01:10.312
The curve is symmetric about that peak, so

00:01:10.412 --> 00:01:12.477
at pi it falls as steeply as it

00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:13.519
rose at zero.

00:01:14.103 --> 00:01:15.477
The slope there is minus one.

00:01:16.184 --> 00:01:18.295
At three pi over two, the trough, it's

00:01:18.336 --> 00:01:18.778
flat again:

00:01:19.342 --> 00:01:19.603
zero.

00:01:20.507 --> 00:01:22.812
And at two pi, the wave starts over,

00:01:23.254 --> 00:01:24.716
climbing with slope one.

00:01:25.702 --> 00:01:27.833
Peaks and troughs are flat, and the crossings

00:01:27.935 --> 00:01:28.577
are the steepest.

00:01:29.256 --> 00:01:31.430
Let's plot those slopes as a new graph

00:01:31.511 --> 00:01:31.853
underneath.

00:01:32.476 --> 00:01:35.184
As the tangent slides along, its gradient draws

00:01:35.205 --> 00:01:35.866
a curve.

00:01:36.489 --> 00:01:38.812
It starts at one, falls to zero at

00:01:38.853 --> 00:01:41.858
the peak, reaches minus one at pi, and

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:42.940
climbs back to one.

00:01:43.663 --> 00:01:46.271
Where sine is rising, the gradient graph sits

00:01:46.412 --> 00:01:48.960
above the axis, and where sine is falling,

00:01:49.302 --> 00:01:50.063
it sits below.

00:01:50.991 --> 00:01:52.643
Check a point that isn't a landmark.

00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:55.735
At pi over three, the measured slope is

00:01:55.776 --> 00:01:57.742
a half, and the cos of pi over

00:01:57.783 --> 00:01:59.348
three is exactly a half.

00:02:00.051 --> 00:02:02.379
This gradient graph is the cosine curve.

00:02:02.942 --> 00:02:05.188
So, with x in radians, the derivative of

00:02:05.269 --> 00:02:06.532
sine x is cos x.

00:02:07.261 --> 00:02:08.729
Why do radians matter?

00:02:09.613 --> 00:02:11.941
On a circle of radius one, an angle

00:02:11.981 --> 00:02:14.310
of x radians cuts off an arc of

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.173
length x.

00:02:15.896 --> 00:02:16.981
That's what a radian means.

00:02:17.806 --> 00:02:19.976
For a small angle, the height, sine x,

00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:21.785
is almost the same as that arc.

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:24.978
So near zero, sine x is close to

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:25.380
x,

00:02:25.962 --> 00:02:27.067
and the slope there is one.

00:02:28.454 --> 00:02:31.506
In degrees, the same graph gets stretched sideways

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:33.133
by about fifty seven point three.

00:02:33.815 --> 00:02:36.621
A sideways stretch divides every slope, at matching

00:02:36.661 --> 00:02:38.125
points, by that number.

00:02:38.928 --> 00:02:40.912
So the slope at zero would be only

00:02:40.973 --> 00:02:42.817
about point zero one seven five.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.956
The rule only works with x in radians.

00:02:46.704 --> 00:02:47.899
Now the cosine curve.

00:02:48.564 --> 00:02:50.435
At zero, it's at a peak, so its

00:02:50.476 --> 00:02:51.321
slope is zero.

00:02:51.924 --> 00:02:52.628
Then it falls,

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.401
steepest at pi over two, where the slope

00:02:55.522 --> 00:02:56.346
is minus one.

00:02:57.088 --> 00:02:58.812
It's flat at the trough, at pi,

00:02:59.313 --> 00:03:01.758
and climbs steepest at three pi over two,

00:03:01.878 --> 00:03:02.720
with slope one.

00:03:03.663 --> 00:03:06.188
Again, the steepest points are the crossings, and

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:07.772
the flat points are the peak and the

00:03:07.834 --> 00:03:08.073
trough.

00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:11.148
Trace those slopes, and you get the sine

00:03:11.189 --> 00:03:12.274
curve, upside down.

00:03:12.978 --> 00:03:15.293
So the derivative of cos x is minus

00:03:15.455 --> 00:03:15.918
sine x.

00:03:16.502 --> 00:03:18.437
Here's why the minus sign belongs there.

00:03:19.323 --> 00:03:21.977
The cosine curve is the sine curve, slid

00:03:22.077 --> 00:03:23.444
left by pi over two.

00:03:24.227 --> 00:03:26.497
A slide carries every tangent along with the

00:03:26.537 --> 00:03:28.485
curve, and doesn't change any slope.

00:03:29.228 --> 00:03:31.775
So the gradient graph slides left by pi

00:03:31.815 --> 00:03:32.738
over two as well.

00:03:33.842 --> 00:03:36.031
Slide the cosine curve left by pi over

00:03:36.072 --> 00:03:38.341
two, and you get the sine curve upside

00:03:38.382 --> 00:03:38.602
down.

00:03:39.549 --> 00:03:40.353
And check the sign.

00:03:40.976 --> 00:03:43.801
Just after zero, cos x is falling, so

00:03:43.842 --> 00:03:46.166
its slope is negative, but sine x is

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:46.908
positive there.

00:03:47.591 --> 00:03:49.699
Plus sine x would give the wrong sign.

00:03:50.316 --> 00:03:52.105
It's worth checking this on a graphing app,

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:53.392
so let's do it.

00:03:54.037 --> 00:03:56.605
Plot sine x, and measure its gradient at

00:03:56.787 --> 00:03:57.448
lots of points.

00:03:58.151 --> 00:04:00.657
Every measured dot lands on the cosine curve,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.201
not just at the landmarks.

00:04:02.807 --> 00:04:04.340
Try a value that isn't special.

00:04:04.965 --> 00:04:07.206
At x equals one, the measured slope is

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:10.108
about point five four, and cos one is

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.269
about point five four too.

00:04:12.053 --> 00:04:13.681
Do the same for cos x, and its

00:04:13.742 --> 00:04:15.691
dots land on minus sine x.

00:04:16.323 --> 00:04:16.846
Let's use it.

00:04:17.551 --> 00:04:20.015
Find every x from zero to two pi

00:04:20.396 --> 00:04:23.321
where y equals three sine x has gradient

00:04:23.522 --> 00:04:24.924
three root two over two.

00:04:26.066 --> 00:04:28.411
Three sine x is the sine curve stretched

00:04:28.571 --> 00:04:30.996
upward by three, so every slope is three

00:04:31.037 --> 00:04:31.658
times as big.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.706
So d y by d x is three

00:04:34.787 --> 00:04:35.368
cos x.

00:04:36.232 --> 00:04:37.996
Set that equal to three root two over

00:04:38.017 --> 00:04:39.400
two, and divide by three.

00:04:40.384 --> 00:04:42.390
Cos x equals root two over two.

00:04:43.254 --> 00:04:45.237
Cos is positive in the first and fourth

00:04:45.298 --> 00:04:48.143
quadrants, so x is pi over four, or

00:04:48.304 --> 00:04:49.265
seven pi over four.

00:04:50.066 --> 00:04:51.708
On the graph, the curve is rising at

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.314
both points, once above the axis and once

00:04:54.355 --> 00:04:56.558
below, and the two tangents are parallel.

00:04:57.522 --> 00:04:59.010
Here's a way to remember the pattern.

00:04:59.714 --> 00:05:02.171
Differentiate sine, and you get cos.

00:05:02.875 --> 00:05:05.505
Differentiate cos, and you get minus sine.

00:05:06.147 --> 00:05:09.150
Keep going: minus sine gives minus cos, and

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:11.252
minus cos gives sine again.

00:05:12.115 --> 00:05:13.929
Four steps, and you're back where you started.

00:05:14.636 --> 00:05:15.623
That matches the graphs.

00:05:16.127 --> 00:05:18.473
Each derivative is the same wave, moved a

00:05:18.595 --> 00:05:20.299
quarter of a period to the left.

00:05:23.105 --> 00:05:23.511
Your turn.

00:05:24.181 --> 00:05:27.025
For f of x equals sine x, find

00:05:27.245 --> 00:05:28.027
f prime of x.

00:05:28.988 --> 00:05:31.110
Then find the exact gradient of the curve

00:05:31.291 --> 00:05:33.393
at x equals two pi over three,

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.496
and at x equals three pi over two.

00:05:37.441 --> 00:05:38.335
Pause here to answer.

00:05:49.124 --> 00:05:50.616
f prime of x is cos x.

00:05:51.543 --> 00:05:53.810
At two pi over three, cos is minus

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.138
a half, so the curve is falling there.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.322
At three pi over two, cos is zero:

00:05:59.963 --> 00:06:01.825
that's the trough, and the curve is flat.

00:06:02.768 --> 00:06:04.716
Minus one is the height at the trough,

00:06:04.876 --> 00:06:05.499
not the gradient.

00:06:07.154 --> 00:06:07.562
Last one.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:10.437
Let g of x equal two cos x,

00:06:10.559 --> 00:06:11.481
plus sine x.

00:06:12.245 --> 00:06:14.613
Find g prime of x, and the gradient

00:06:14.715 --> 00:06:16.721
of the tangent at x equals pi over

00:06:16.763 --> 00:06:16.942
two.

00:06:17.691 --> 00:06:18.459
Pause here to answer.

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:30.643
Differentiate each term.

00:06:31.327 --> 00:06:33.716
Two cos x gives minus two sine x,

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.087
and sine x gives cos x.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.471
At pi over two, sine is one and

00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:42.213
cos is zero, so the gradient is minus

00:06:42.275 --> 00:06:42.514
two.

00:06:43.418 --> 00:06:45.248
If you lost the minus sign from cos,

00:06:45.631 --> 00:06:46.716
you'd get plus two.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.886
But two cos x is falling at pi

00:06:49.907 --> 00:06:52.232
over two, so the gradient must be negative.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.483
So the sine curve's slopes trace the cosine

00:06:56.503 --> 00:06:56.783
curve,

00:06:57.323 --> 00:07:00.807
and cosine's slopes trace minus sine, with x

00:07:00.867 --> 00:07:01.427
in radians.

00:07:02.449 --> 00:07:04.838
Next, what happens when there's something inside?

00:07:05.763 --> 00:07:06.689
Here's sine x,

00:07:07.253 --> 00:07:08.320
and sine two x.

00:07:09.045 --> 00:07:11.343
Sine two x leaves the origin more steeply.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:12.612
By how much,

00:07:13.277 --> 00:07:13.698
and why?

00:07:16.328 --> 00:07:17.191
Here are the key notes.

00:07:17.594 --> 00:07:19.141
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:19.183 --> 00:07:19.644
to copy them.
